          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-2c384c3fbd203755\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Armand directly avows that his love for Marguerite persists despite her apparent betrayal. The clause primarily presents his continuing emotional state as true. Its use while preventing her departure and seeking reconciliation gives it slight commitment-related force, but the love clause itself does not clearly undertake an obligation. The scene's intensity does not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"There is limited P/T ambiguity because the avowal helps initiate reconciliation, but the explicit undertaking appears mainly in the adjacent promises rather than in “je t’aime toujours” itself.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Armand speaks “je t’aime toujours” directly to Marguerite in dialogue, as narrated retrospectively in the first person.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Marguerite is leaving after Armand accuses her of lying; moved by her visible suffering, he blocks the door and explains that he still loves her and wants to keep her there. The avowal reverses his hostile posture and contributes to an attempted reconciliation, although his jealousy and possessiveness complicate its relational meaning. His subsequent promise to forget everything provides stronger performative evidence for the surrounding exchange than the highlighted clause itself does.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Parce que, malgré ce que tu m'as fait, je t'aime toujours”\",\"supports\":\"The concessive phrase and “toujours” explicitly present Armand's love as a continuing state despite perceived injury, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Armand says this after placing himself before the door when Marguerite tries to leave, adding “je veux te garder ici.”\",\"supports\":\"The avowal functions within an attempt to retain Marguerite and restore intimacy, providing weak secondary support for P while also showing the possessive context.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately afterward Armand says, “j'oublierai tout, et nous serons heureux comme nous nous étions promis de l'être.”\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding exchange contains an explicit future-oriented undertaking, but its separation from the highlighted love clause limits the latter's P score.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Before the avowal, Armand reports being moved by Marguerite's “muette et expressive douleur”; the later “m'écriai-je” introduces a subsequent utterance, not the highlighted one.\",\"supports\":\"The scene is emotionally intense, but it supplies no positive evidence that the highlighted words themselves are a reflex-like expressive discharge; this supports E=0.\",\"confidence\":0.93}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the persistent-state avowal, with slight P support from its reconciliatory use. Contextual intensity, jealousy, and possessiveness do not create residual core force requiring E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
